education.narrative:
Japan has had 82 medical schools since 2017. No new schools opened from 1979 under a policy to limit the number of physicians, until Tohoku Medical and Pharmaceutical University opened a medical school in 2016 and the International University of Health and Welfare in Narita City, Chiba, the newest, opened in April 2017 as the 82nd.{{2}} The Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology regulates them.{{1}} The standard undergraduate program is six years, typically four years of preclinical and two of clinical education, and high school graduates are eligible to enter.{{1}}

Graduates take the National Medical Practitioners Examination once a year; the 120th sitting, held on 7 and 8 February 2026, drew 9,980 candidates with a 91.6 percent pass rate, and the examination has 400 questions.{{3}}{{4}} The law requires two years of postgraduate training, with a residency matching system that began in 2004.{{1}} Those details come from a 2006 review, and a 2023 account still describes a national licensure exam and a residency matching process.{{2}}

On 23 April 2026 the Finance Ministry told its advisory council that doctor supply and demand should balance around 2029 to 2032 and that a future surplus is already certain given the six-year degree, and it urged a bold, planned cut in medical school capacity.{{5}}
